@@ -6,6 +6,61 @@ This chapter documents the hook system architecture, all seventeen hook scripts,
 
 ---
 
+## Copy-channel preapproval policy
+
+`core/tools/aidlc-copy-preapproval.ts` owns the pure selection of tool scripts
+and exact dispatcher command spellings that a copy-channel install preapproves.
+The dispatcher supplies route mutation scopes, tool names, configuration sections
+and recorded project bypass names; it retains route discovery and lazy settings
+loading. Host-specific permission rendering stays with each harness.
+
+A script used by any machine or project-and-machine route is excluded even when
+another route uses it for project work. Dispatcher approval includes the declared
+read-only utility forms and restoring a recorded check with `--clear-bypass`;
+guided configuration and other configuration writes still use the host's prompt.
+Selection does not itself execute commands or grant host authority. Co-located
+tests cover this contract through the standard unit runner.
+
+## Hook runtime location policy
+
+`core/tools/aidlc-hook-location-policy.ts` plans which runtime locations may
+supply a hook. Native hooks use the packaged path even when that runtime is
+damaged; the caller reports the missing file rather than executing a project
+copy. The customizable statusline searches project locations first. Copy-channel
+hooks search beside the dispatcher, then project locations, then the packaged
+location. The dispatcher retains path discovery and probes candidates in order,
+stopping at the first existing file. The policy performs no filesystem reads.
+
+## Machine transaction parent
+
+`core/tools/aidlc-machine-transaction-parent.ts` selects the shared parent for
+machine transaction locks, journals and rollback records from decoded install
+and command directory roots and segments, without platform dependencies. The
+install-path facade retains configured-root discovery, canonicalization, path
+decoding and joining. Different parsed filesystem roots, or a common parent equal
+to the filesystem root, retain their existing refusals. Windows drive, share and
+segment comparisons retain their existing case-sensitive spelling rules.
+The facade also checks the joined path: legal POSIX backslash names can decode
+into segments that native joining collapses to the filesystem root.
+
+This selects a transaction scope; it does not establish writability, prove that
+paths are on the same physical device, or authorize mutations of other files
+under the shared parent. Existing operation checks and transaction execution
+retain those responsibilities.
+
+## Installed runtime inventory
+
+`core/tools/aidlc-runtime-inventory.ts` compares the recorded file inventory
+against collected runtime files. Count, baseline entry shape, path order, mode
+and hash retain their existing diagnostic priority; the first mismatch wins.
+The policy does not sort, deduplicate, repair files or introduce new path rules.
+
+`inspectInstalledVersion` retains manifest identity checks, filesystem access,
+hash collection, compatibility decisions and distribution admission. It collects
+the complete observed inventory before comparing it. The comparator consumes
+parsed baseline entries and the collector's ordinary file records; it does not
+provide a general interface for lazy filesystem access or proxy-backed arrays.
+
